<article class="container pt-10 pb-5">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <h1 class="pb-3">Azure Infrastructure</h1>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Three Terraform layers</h2>
            <p>
                I split the infrastructure across three repositories.
                Platform.Subscription defines shared subscription resources, including a Key Vault and access assignments.
                Platform.Environment defines the shared Azure network, Kubernetes cluster, container registry and SQL server for an environment.
                Platform.App defines resources for each application environment, including its database, storage and Kubernetes namespace.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">From plan to deployment</h2>
            <p>
                Azure DevOps uses a shared Terraform template across the three repositories.
                The target selects its own variables and Azure Blob Storage state file.
                The pipeline creates a plan and applies that saved plan when the deployment is enabled and there are changes.
                When work spans all three layers, the order is Subscription, Environment, then App.
                The App pipeline then uses Helm to release Blogs, Identity and Web.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Connecting Azure to the HomeLab</h2>
            <p>
                The dev sites use an Istio gateway on AKS with a private frontend.
                The reverse proxy at home reaches it over WireGuard.
                The Environment layer owns the Istio control plane, while the App layer owns the dev gateway release.
                That split lets me deploy changes to the application's routing independently of changes to the cluster's Istio installation.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
